# Direct dependencies of the recon SOLVE environment in the GPU image
# (enrich/recon/Dockerfile.gpu), mirroring scripts/enrich/.venv, which has no lockfile
# of its own. Versions are the ones that produced every archived run: the GPU port must
# change ONE variable, the device, or the CPU-vs-GPU comparison proves nothing.
#
# TORCH IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. The CUDA build is not on PyPI, so the Dockerfile installs
# it once, system-wide, from the CUDA wheel index; this venv is created with
# --system-site-packages and sees it. That also stops the image carrying two ~3 GB copies
# of torch, one here and one in the segmentation environment.
#
# ...but absent from the .in file is not enough: kornia DEPENDS on torch, so a plain
# compile resolves it from PyPI, whose torch 2.12.x is the cu130 build, and drags in
# fifteen nvidia-*-cu13 wheels with it. The venv then shadows the system cu126 torch and
# curope refuses to compile: "detected CUDA version (12.6) mismatches the version that was
# used to compile PyTorch (13.0)". So the whole CUDA stack is kept OUT of the lock with
# --no-emit-package, and the venvs (built --system-site-packages) use the one torch the
# Dockerfile installed. Anything satisfied by the environment is never downloaded, so
# --require-hashes stays happy.
#
# Regenerate after edits, with the repo's cooloff convention (scripts/enrich/README.md):
#   EXCL=""; for p in torch triton cuda-bindings cuda-pathfinder cuda-toolkit \
#       nvidia-cublas nvidia-cuda-cupti nvidia-cuda-nvrtc nvidia-cuda-runtime \
#       nvidia-cudnn-cu13 nvidia-cufft nvidia-cufile nvidia-curand nvidia-cusolver \
#       nvidia-cusparse nvidia-cusparselt-cu13 nvidia-nccl-cu13 nvidia-nvjitlink \
#       nvidia-nvshmem-cu13 nvidia-nvtx; do EXCL="$EXCL --no-emit-package $p"; done
#   uv pip compile --generate-hashes --python-version 3.12 \
#       --exclude-newer "$(date -d '6 weeks ago' +%F)" $EXCL \
#       requirements-recon.in -o requirements-recon.txt
